/// `std-authority`: The ambient authority.
/// Implemented: `std.authority`, `library/std/src/authority.gandr`, names `Authority`, a capability holding every host operation a landed module of `std` reaches, which `main` takes as its parameter, `def main(authority : Authority)`, and `gandr run` hands it once, a capability no handler of the run introduced, so each exercise through it is the host's; `console` opens the standard streams' root capability from it by attenuation.
/// Not implemented: a consumable capability, `Cap[1]`, so the authority is durable, not linear. It holds the standard streams' operations alone until `std.fs`, `std.net`, `std.time`, `std.rand`, `std.process` and `std.env` land. An exercise on a value typed by the alias, `authority.print(…)`, is refused as a projection, so a program exercises it through a parameter of capability type.
///
/// - owes: #3509 — a consumable capability, `Cap[1]`
/// - owes: #1106 — an exercise on a value typed by the alias
/// - see: #2970

import std.authority (Authority, console);
// The console's host interface.
import std.io;

/// A greeting, printed through a capability that holds `print` alone.
pub def greet(console : Cap { print }) -> [std.io.Console.Eff] Unit =
  console.print "hello, world!"

/// The greeting through the console that `main` opens from its authority.
pub def hello(authority : Authority) -> [std.io.Console.Eff] Unit =
  greet (console authority)
